WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:10.349
Dear friends of VisualPolitik.

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:12.940
Bolivia has recently undergone a whirlwind
of events.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:18.150
Elections, electoral fraud, civil revolution
and finally, something that seemed impossible:

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.220
The fall of Evo Morales.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.660
We’re going to tell you the story of how
Evo Morales went from having all the power,

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.680
to giving up the presidency and even being
on the run and caught.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.380
Listen up.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:37.770
On October 20th, 2019, general elections were
held in the country.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:42.030
That night, after the results were announced,
social peace exploded.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:45.879
Since then the protests and demonstrations
of both the opposition and the supporters

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:50.329
of the ruling party, Evo Morales’s supporters,
occurred relentlessly in the country’s main

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:53.409
cities in what some call the “Bolivian spring”.

00:00:53.409 --> 00:00:59.780
(AUDIO: The La Paz airport turned into a battlefield,
a city hall devoured by flames and tear gas

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.239
shot at the innumerable blocking points installed
throughout the country.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:04.239
BBC)

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.440
The situation was so tense that there was
even a police riot.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:11.040
(AUDIO: Police riot against Evo Morales, who
denounces a coup d'etat.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.280
The first to rebel was the Tactical Unit of
Police Operations in the central city of Cochabamba,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.930
and shortly after other cities’ commands
rioted.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:19.930
El Comercio)

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.100
All this is related to something we already
predicted, here on VisualPolitik.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.549
We told you how Evo Morales had fallen in
love with power, how he’d ignored the current

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:31.909
law in order to run for president again, and
how everything indicated that he’d stop

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:38.119
at nothing to stay in power.

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:42.789
Even if that meant following the rule-by-force
strategy that has kept characters like Nicolás

00:01:42.789 --> 00:01:45.960
Maduro, Daniel Ortega or Juan Orlando Hernández
in power.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.380
Power at any price.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.820
Well, that's exactly what’s happened.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:02:00.640
But having said that, let’s see exactly
how events unfolded in Bolivia.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:11.280
(A FORETOLD STORY)

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.310
This whole story began on February 21st, 2016.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:22.170
That day, 51% of Bolivians rejected the modification
of article 168 of the Constitution in a referendum.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.750
This Constitution, which clearly prohibits
the re-election of an individual for more

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.840
than one term of office, was approved at Evo
Morales’s own request.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.000
(AUDIO: “The President and Vice President’s
term of office is 5 years and he or she may

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.720
only be reelected once continuously.”

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.890
Article 168 of the Bolivian Constitution)

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:45.600
Faced with this situation, with a Constitution
that you yourself approved and that is so

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.740
clearly laid out, and having lost a referendum
to modify it, you’d probably say...

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:53.930
Ok, I respect the rules, another candidate
should run for my party.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:54.930
Right?

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.980
At least that’s what happens in every country
with a minimum respect for the law.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:02:59.980
Well...

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:00.980
, Evo wouldn’t.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:01.980
Leave power?

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.380
No way.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:11.200
And, as in every country where the government
has power over all the institutions, it didn’t

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.450
aim to work for the public good, but for the
good of the government itself.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:26.380
Thus, in 2017, the Plurinational Bolivian
Constitutional Court published the grotesque

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:32.010
0084/2017 judgment by which articles of the
Constitution itself were declared unconstitutional,

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:37.500
which included article 168, therefore allowing
for indefinite reelections.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.040
How?

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:46.510
Welcome to Evo Morales’s Bolivia.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:50.920
The fact is that the Constitutional Court’s
controversial decision was a huge blow to

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.231
Bolivia’s rule of law: in the end it didn’t
matter what the Constitution said or what

00:03:55.231 --> 00:03:57.180
the Bolivians themselves voted for.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.370
The only thing that mattered was what Evo
Morales wanted.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:04.340
And faced with this situation, large numbers
of Bolivian society took to the streets to

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:09.880
protest under the cry “Bolivia said No”.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:19.310
But... here’s another question, do you think
this affected Evo Morales at all?

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:23.590
Did it make him rethink his position or try
to reach an understanding with most of Bolivia’s

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.520
society who had voted against his re-election?

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.520
Well...

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:28.520
Obviously not.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.970
Evo made it clear that what he wants is power...

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.070
At any cost.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:45.360
[TEXT: That’s why we’re here with a political
liberation project, with a people government

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.360
program.]

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.720
But, things did start changing at that time.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.520
The government had played fair and lost the
referendum.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.470
So they made sure that when the time came,
they wouldn’t lose at the polls again.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:56.470
And you know what?

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:57.500
It was a done deal.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:02.060
From that moment strange, abnormal, unheard
of situations began to happen.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:10.460
Check it out.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:14.970
Many members and officials of the Supreme
Electoral Tribunal resigned due to the government’s

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:19.650
very strong pressure; there had been a very
suspicious electoral roll increase of 190%

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:22.620
between 2005 and 2019.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:27.290
A spectacular growth that evidently doesn’t
match population growth for the same period

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:28.290
of time.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:34.560
And things got even worse as the elections
approached.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:50.830
The ruling party, the MAS, the 
Movement towards Socialism, didn’t hesitate

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:55.330
to use State resources to campaign, without
the electoral authorities saying anything

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:56.330
of course.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:59.740
Meanwhile these same authorities were busy
censoring independent surveys that didn’t

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:01.270
declare Evo Morales a winner.

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:05.370
For example, that happened with the surveys
prepared by the Higher University of San Andrés.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:09.280
Just a second, why would the government censor
surveys?

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:12.669
Think about it for a moment, if you’re going
to commit fraud, you want to make sure it’s

00:06:12.669 --> 00:06:16.720
not too scandalous, right?

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:22.640
On top of that, the only company authorized
to perform the quick vote count on election

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.400
day was Viaciencia, a company that’s less
than a year old.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.700
That sounds like a good idea; giving control
of a large and complex electoral process to

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:33.070
a newly created and inexperienced company.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.610
And there you have it.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:38.070
The road towards electoral fraud was paved.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.130
So do you want to know exactly how Evo Morales
stole the elections?

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.130
Well...

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:44.220
Listen up.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.900
(THE GREAT HEIST)

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:55.630
The Supreme Electoral Tribunal, Bolivia’s
highest electoral authority, promised to deliver

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:58.949
100% of the quick count results by eight o'clock
on election night.

00:06:58.949 --> 00:06:59.949
Well...

00:06:59.949 --> 00:07:00.949
Do you know what happened?

00:07:00.949 --> 00:07:04.690
When the time came, the Electoral Tribunal
delivered 83% of the count results, not all

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:13.890
of them, and the electoral results broadcast
was suspended without any notice or clarification.

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:17.860
By that time, the preliminary results marked
a clear trend: Evo Morales was winning in

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:23.389
the first round with 45.28% over Carlos Mesa,
the main opposition candidate, who’d obtained

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:24.389
38.16%.

00:07:24.389 --> 00:07:29.699
Therefore, there was only a 7.12% difference
between the two candidates which, according

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:37.139
to article 167 of the Constitution, would
lead to a second round.

00:07:37.139 --> 00:07:50.139
You see, article 167 of the Bolivian Constitution
states that the candidate who gets 50% of

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:51.650
the votes will be declared President.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:55.940
Alternatively, if a candidate gets more than
40% of the vote with 10 points ahead of the

00:07:55.940 --> 00:08:00.410
candidate with the second highest votes, he
or she can also be declared president.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:03.840
If these conditions aren’t met, a second
round must be held.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.320
A second round in which, given the results,
victory wasn’t quite so certain for Evo

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.790
Morales.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:16.810
However, we’ve already told you that Evo
wasn’t willing to lose power.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:18.910
What’s more, he had a plan.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:23.270
The following day, and after more than 17
hours of unexplained suspended coverage, the

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:28.710
Fast-Preliminary Results Transmission jumped
from 83% to 95%, only to be suspended again

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:39.329
until finally five, five days later, 100%
of the official vote count was reached.

00:08:39.329 --> 00:08:42.259
And surprise, surprise, the results were suddenly
very different.

00:08:42.259 --> 00:08:47.369
There was to be no second round, Evo had beat
his rival by more than 10 points.

00:08:47.369 --> 00:08:54.589
And that’s when everything kicked off.

00:08:54.589 --> 00:08:58.360
Thousands and thousands of citizens began
to amass in front of the Electoral Tribunal

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.730
and suspicion of fraud ran wild.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:06.309
No survey, no poll nor any statistical program
had foreseen such a vote deviation.

00:09:06.309 --> 00:09:10.420
A deviation at the end of the count that was
completely opaque and happened amid serious

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:31.069
irregularities in the counting process.

00:09:31.069 --> 00:09:35.119
This is what led to protests throughout the
country, protests that fundamentally demanded

00:09:35.119 --> 00:09:37.310
one thing: clean elections.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:40.869
As you can imagine the government’s first
reaction was to try to stifle the protests

00:09:40.869 --> 00:09:43.220
through repression using the state security
forces.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:44.600
And that was that.

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:48.819
Far from seeking a solution to the problem,
what Evo did was to denounce an alleged coup

00:09:48.819 --> 00:09:53.689
against him, against him, a president who
didn’t hesitate to ignore his own constitution

00:09:53.689 --> 00:09:58.569
and deploy all state institutions for his
personal service.

00:09:58.569 --> 00:10:04.700
Now, at this point some of you may be thinking….

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:08.759
But, Simon, was the fraud, the electoral robbery,
really so blatant?

00:10:08.759 --> 00:10:16.259
Well, frankly, everything points to yes.

00:10:16.259 --> 00:10:19.069
Practically all the independent players agree
on this.

00:10:19.069 --> 00:10:23.370
For example, international organizations that
acted as observers during the elections, such

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:27.300
as the European Union or the Organization
of American States observers, have recommended

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:31.329
holding a second round because the official
result cannot be accepted.

00:10:31.329 --> 00:10:36.129
(AUDIO: “Every election must be governed
by the principles of certainty, legality,

00:10:36.129 --> 00:10:41.600
transparency, fairness, independence and impartiality...
the Mission was able to verify that several

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.810
of these principles have been violated through
different paths throughout this electoral

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:47.810
process.”

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:50.379
Report from the Organization of American States
Observers.)

00:10:50.379 --> 00:10:52.069
And that’s not all.

00:10:52.069 --> 00:10:54.949
Even civil society members spotted a lot of
irregularities.

00:10:54.949 --> 00:10:59.050
For example, there’s the case of the computer
systems engineer Edgar Villegas who, along

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:03.189
with a team of professionals, has worked on
comparing the official calculation tables

00:11:03.189 --> 00:11:05.869
with the results transmission.

00:11:05.869 --> 00:11:09.899
His conclusion was that the process was full
of traps, which included the elimination of

00:11:09.899 --> 00:11:13.649
voting results tables, to favor MAS, Evo Morales’s
party.

00:11:13.649 --> 00:11:15.300
And there’s more, check this out:

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:20.670
(AUDIO: Bolivia: the official audit described
the elections as “null and void” and Carlos

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:22.709
Mesa demanded new elections.

00:11:22.709 --> 00:11:27.300
The company hired by the Supreme Electoral
Tribunal delivered a report stating that it

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:34.360
cannot “attest to the integrity of the results”
of the presidential elections held last October

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:35.360
20th.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:36.360
InfoBae)

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.180
As you can see, not even the company that
the Electoral Tribunal itself hired, which

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:42.870
has fully backed Evo in the past, dares to
validate the results.

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:45.980
The fraud is too evident.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:52.230
And finally, the Organization of American
States observers’ preliminary report was

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:57.730
very much in the same vein: fraud, deceit,
scams, a mockery of the popular will, all

00:11:57.730 --> 00:11:58.759
of this was evident.

00:11:58.759 --> 00:12:03.550
Evo Morales wanted to become a leader, just
like his friends Nicolas Maduro or Daniel

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:26.779
Ortega, who as you all know are experts in
ignoring the democratic process.

00:12:26.779 --> 00:12:30.559
Anyone who’s been following Bolivia’s
political news knows one thing: Evo Morales

00:12:30.559 --> 00:12:32.570
was going to try to stay in power no matter
what.

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:36.869
However, the street protesters didn’t want
to see Evo become a new Nicolas Maduro and

00:12:36.869 --> 00:12:41.709
the bungling and scandalous way in which he
tried to perpetrate electoral robbery meant

00:12:41.709 --> 00:12:43.059
his shot backfired.

00:12:43.059 --> 00:12:47.089
The police began to mutiny so they wouldn’t
have to comply with the government's repression

00:12:47.089 --> 00:12:48.089
orders.

00:12:48.089 --> 00:12:51.179
And that, along with the mass protests that
were taking place in the country, led Evo

00:12:51.179 --> 00:12:53.819
to announce a call for new elections.

00:12:53.819 --> 00:13:03.279
[TEXT: “Calling for new national elections
that, through the vote, allow the Bolivian

00:13:03.279 --> 00:13:09.509
people to democratically elect their new authorities”]

00:13:09.509 --> 00:13:13.410
But was the same person who committed the
fraud going to organize and stand for election

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:14.410
again?

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:16.369
What credibility would that process have?

00:13:16.369 --> 00:13:18.920
Merely repeating the elections was no longer
enough.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:22.230
The people on the streets, the opposition
parties, the independent media...

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:26.440
All the forces outside MAS had begun demanding
the President’s resignation.

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:36.860
And finally, the army followed suit:

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:40.470
In fact, that was the last straw; without
the people, without the police and without

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:45.100
the army, Evo Morales was forced to hand in
his resignation and resign from Bolivia’s

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:46.100
presidency.

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:51.230
(AUDIO: Bolivia’s Morales resigns amid scathing
election report, rising protests.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:52.230
Washington Post)

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:54.449
But... just a second, because the drama didn’t
end there.

00:13:54.449 --> 00:13:55.749
This is what followed:

00:13:55.749 --> 00:14:01.860
(AUDIO: Evo Morales travels to Mexico on a
plane owned by the López Obrador Government.

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:04.790
- France 24)

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:08.730
And so, that’s how Evo Morales’s time
in Bolivia came to an end.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:12.629
After his departure, the new transition government
has announced a call for new elections, elections

00:14:12.629 --> 00:14:15.069
that will be supervised by international organizations.

00:14:15.069 --> 00:14:19.759
Fine, the procedure may not have been the
best, but Bolivian democracy was at stake.

00:14:19.759 --> 00:14:23.889
Evo would’ve had to leave, but he got drunk
with power and decided to turn Bolivia into

00:14:23.889 --> 00:14:25.299
his own personal playground.

00:14:25.299 --> 00:14:26.540
Of course, it doesn't end there.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:31.630
Bolivia now faces not only a deep institutional
conflict but also significant economic imbalances.

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:35.350
Evo’s most recent years were bad, very bad,
and the future is far from clear.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:37.839
The country needs a complete change of course.

00:14:37.839 --> 00:14:40.009
But we’ll talk about all of that in an upcoming
video.

00:14:40.009 --> 00:14:41.029
So now, it’s your turn.

00:14:41.029 --> 00:14:43.509
Do you think Evo's departure will be good
for Bolivia?

00:14:43.509 --> 00:14:46.360
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:50.499
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:14:50.499 --> 00:14:51.920
subscribe for brand new videos.

